---
description: Materialize an approved architecture and planner decomposition into a private Beads epic
{{- if .modules.opencode }}
agent: orchestrator
{{- end }}
---

Materialize the existing canonical epic `$1` and nothing else.

Load the `beads-hub` skill before acting. Follow its contract and use only `wbd` for Hub operations. This command accepts exactly one positional argument: if `$1` is missing or `$2` is present, stop and report the usage error without running any `wbd` command. The argument must be the exact canonical ID supplied by the user; never resolve, shorten, or guess an ID.

Use `history-search` and `read_session` to locate and read the completed architect and planner lane outputs for this repository. Do not require those outputs to be retained in the current command context or already separated. Require exactly one completed, explicitly approved architect design and its corresponding completed planner decomposition; stop before any Hub mutation if either output is missing, incomplete, or ambiguous. Extract the approved architecture verbatim and the planner slices from those lane outputs without asking the user to supply or reorganize them. Do not launch, ask for, redo, infer, summarize, repair, or alter architecture or planner work.

Before mutating anything, run `wbd show "$1" --json` and require one exact returned record whose `issue_type` is `epic`. Then run `wbd comments "$1" --json` and `wbd show "$1" --json --expand-dependencies`; inspect all existing comments and direct `parent-child` children. Compare every existing comment body's text byte-for-byte with the retrieved approved architecture. Stop and report if a comment matches that architecture exactly, or if any direct `parent-child` child exists. Ignore unrelated comments; they do not indicate materialization. Because this workflow has no ownership marker, do not use heuristics or attempt to distinguish, repair, retry, roll back, or delete a materialization. Do not duplicate anything.

Before the first mutation, adapt and validate every planner slice conservatively. Require at least one distinct slice with a non-empty heading or name and a complete body. Use an explicit title or otherwise the heading/name as the title, and use an explicit description or otherwise the complete slice body as the description. Explicit Beads values win; otherwise default type to `task`, priority to `2`, blockers to none, and context to the current repository's epic context only after confirming that context belongs to the target epic. Require exactly one valid context and a concrete type (`task`, `bug`, `feature`, or `chore`) for every adapted slice. Reject malformed or duplicate slices and missing, duplicate, self-referential, or unknown slice references. Add a blocking edge only when the planner explicitly marks a listed blocker as genuine; never turn a suggestion, ordering preference, architecture relationship, or slice order into a dependency.

Inspect active scope state during preflight. If one active scope exists, add each child to it after validating the child; if no active scope exists, skip scope membership. Stop before mutation if active scope state is ambiguous or invalid. Never create or activate a scope.

After all preflight checks pass, perform mutations sequentially and stop on the first failed mutation:

1. Preserve the approved architecture exactly, byte-for-byte, as one comment on the epic. Put only that architecture text in a mode-0600 temporary runtime file, with no title, heading, summary, or wrapper prose, and run `wbd comments add "$1" --file "$architecture_file" --json`. Remove the file afterward. Do not put its contents or private IDs/contexts in any Git-visible file.
2. For each planner slice, run `wbd create "$title" --type "$type" --description "$description" --priority "$priority" --context "$context" --json`. Parse exactly one returned child ID, then validate it immediately with `wbd show "$child_id" --json`: it must be that exact canonical ID, a concrete child of the requested type, with the approved title, description, priority, and exactly one approved context. Record the child ID only in runtime state.
3. If preflight found one active scope, add that validated child to it with its context using `wbd scope add "$child_id" --context "$context" --json`. Parse the returned JSON and require `operation` equal to `add`, `matched` equal to `1`, and `changed` equal to `1`; otherwise stop immediately. If preflight found no active scope, skip this step.
4. After the conditional scope step, parent-link that validated child with `wbd dep add "$child_id" "$1" --type parent-child --json`. The child is the dependent and `$1` is its epic parent; this is the only lifecycle relationship to the epic. Do not use `wbd link`, claim, close, or any deletion operation.
5. After every slice has been created, conditionally scope-added, and parent-linked, add only the explicitly approved genuine blocker edges with `wbd dep add "$blocked_child_id" "$blocker_child_id" --type blocks --json`, in planner order. Do not add any other edge.

On any failed mutation or returned-child validation, stop immediately. Do not retry, rollback, delete, claim, close, link, launch workers, or begin implementation. Report the blocker and the completed versus unfinished operations, including only runtime IDs and contexts in that report.
